WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.000
Before assigning GPU resources, teams should classify the workload.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.250
Not every GPU job has the same purpose.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.000
Development notebooks, quick experiments, scheduled training, batch evaluation,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
data processing, production inference,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.995
and urgent operational work each deserve different cost controls.

00:00:14.995 --> 00:00:19.000
Experiments should usually be easy to start, time-box, observe, and clean up.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.000
The goal is learning.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.000
If an experiment becomes important,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.225
it can be promoted into a more durable workflow with better tracking.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:26.000
Until then,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.520
default limits and automatic shutdown reduce the chance that exploration becomes unmanaged

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.205
spend.

00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:32.000
Training jobs need stronger reproducibility.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.190
Teams should know the dataset version, code version, configuration, checkpoint plan,

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.000
success criteria, and stop condition.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.080
A job that cannot be reproduced or evaluated may consume budget without producing a

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:42.995
reliable decision.

00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:45.215
Inference workloads have a different cost shape.

00:00:45.215 --> 00:00:50.000
Spend may follow traffic, latency goals, model size, batch behavior, scaling policy,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:50.995
and uptime expectations.

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:55.923
The cost target should be connected to service value, not only to hardware utilization.

00:00:55.923 --> 00:00:58.880
Production resources should have clear owners, tags, approval paths,

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.255
and review expectations.

00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:02.995
Classification lets teams tie controls to business intent.

00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:07.740
The result is a more useful policy than treating every GPU allocation as if it had the

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.255
same risk and value.

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:11.000
Classification also helps during review.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Instead of asking whether a job is expensive in the abstract,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
teams can ask whether the spend matches the workload class, the expected learning,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
the service requirement, or the business outcome.
